{
  "decision": "map_front",
  "rationale": "The feasible front is narrow and entirely one family: all 13 Pareto designs are `iterative`, spanning area_um2 7149..12834 and accuracy_bits 10.2..17.6, while the registry permits data_width up to 28 and n_iter up to 30 (so accuracy well beyond 17.6 bits is reachable) and the HV reference area is 40000. The front therefore covers only a small slice of the accuracy objective's range, and the low-area corner (7149 um2, accuracy 10.2 bits) sits right against the 2^-10 constraint, so there is a narrow unexplored window between 10.0 and 10.2 bits where a smaller-area design could still be feasible. With 100 evals left in the budget, the right move is to let NSGA-II map the whole front over the full ranges of the families already on it (iterative, and unrolled_k which had 4 feasible designs but none on the front), seeded with the current front — this both pushes the low-area corner down and extends the high-accuracy end, maximising hypervolume coverage rather than over-focusing on one corner.",
  "next_plan": null
}

{
  "decision": "refine",
  "next_plan": {
    "families": [
      {
        "budget_share": 0.65,
        "family": "iterative",
        "ranges": [
          {"choices": ["iterative"], "high": 17, "low": 12, "param": "data_width"},
          {"choices": ["iterative"], "high": 22, "low": 13, "param": "n_iter"},
          {"choices": ["iterative"], "high": 3, "low": -2, "param": "angle_guard"},
          {"choices": ["iterative"], "high": 3, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"},
          {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"}
        ]
      , "why": "Low-area corner: the selection rule is min area_um2 and the front's cheapest point (W=16,n=15 -> 7149) sits only 0.24 bit above the 2^-10 error constraint, so the true minimum-area feasible point is very likely just below it in width. data_width=14 was already seen feasible, so 12..17 will pin the feasibility boundary in W and find designs cheaper than 7149."},
      {
        "budget_share": 0.35,
        "family": "iterative",
        "ranges": [
          {"choices": ["iterative"], "high": 28, "low": 21, "param": "data_width"},
          {"choices": ["iterative"], "high": 30, "low": 21, "param": "n_iter"},
          {"choices": ["iterative"], "high": 4, "low": -2, "param": "angle_guard"},
          {"choices": ["iterative"], "high": 4, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"},
          {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"}
        ]
      , "why": "High-accuracy tail: the front stops at 20.8 bits (W=26,n=26) while the registry allows W=28,N=30, so the top of the accuracy objective is unmapped; extending it is the cheapest remaining hypervolume (HV ref accuracy is only 10 bits, so every extra bit above 10 adds dominated volume)."}
    ]
  ,
  "rationale": "The feasible front is 100% `iterative`; `unrolled_k` was already sampled (15 evals) and is fully dominated on this spec (best feasible area 12012 > 7149, best accuracy 11.42 << 20.76). The throughput constraint is a non-issue (0% violations, worst front point still 3.12 MSPS vs the 1 MSPS floor), so pipelined/pipelined_m and higher k cannot help an area-first spec. Two things are still loose: (1) the min-area corner, where the current winner (7149 um^2, 10.24 bits) sits only 0.24 bit inside the error constraint and data_width=14 already proved feasible, so the true area minimum is unpinned; (2) the accuracy ceiling, where the front ends at 20.8 bits against a registry limit of W=28/N=30, leaving HV on the table. Refining those two ends is better use of the 80 remaining evals than another broad map, and the reserved end-of-run mapping pass can still cover the full front afterwards."
} 

,
"rationale": "Front is entirely `iterative`; unrolled_k is dominated (12012 um^2 / 11.42 bits) and throughput is not binding (0% violations, min front throughput 3.12 MSPS >> 1 MSPS), so adding pipelined families would only inflate area. Refine both ends of the front instead."
